.SH NAME
mocp \- copy files through a mosh session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mocp
.RI [ options ]
\fISOURCE\fP...
\fIDESTINATION\fP
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmocp\fP copies files between this machine and another, as
.BR scp (1)
does, through a
.BR mosh (1)
session instead of an ssh connection.  The copy therefore survives what
a mosh session survives: losing the network, changing networks, the
machine sleeping.  It waits, and carries on.  If \fBmocp\fP itself is
stopped part way, running it again continues from where it got to.

Experimental, and part of an unofficial fork of mosh.  It needs this
build of mosh (1.4.0-pf.4 or later) at both ends, and a login to the
other machine by ssh, which is how the session is started.

Exactly one end of a copy is the other machine, written
[\fIuser\fP@]\fIhost\fP:\fIpath\fP.  A relative \fIpath\fP is relative
to the home directory there, and \fB~/\fP means the same.  A name with
a colon after a slash, or after a single letter, is a local file; write
\fB./a:b\fP for a local file whose name has a colon in it.

With several sources, or a destination that is a directory or ends in
\fB/\fP, the sources are put in the destination under their own names.
Otherwise the one source takes the destination as its name.  A source
directory given as \fIdir\fP\fB/.\fP means what is in it, not the
directory itself.  Patterns in remote sources (\fBhost:logs/*.txt\fP;
quote them from your own shell) are expanded on the other machine.

A file arrives under a temporary name
(\fB.\fP\fIname\fP\fB.mocp-part\fP) and replaces the file of its own
name only when all of it has arrived and its checksum agrees, so a
reader of the destination never sees half a file.  A file that is
already at the destination with the same size and modification time is
not sent again.  Modification times and permission bits are kept (the
latter subject to the umask at the destination).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Copy directories, and everything in them.  Links to directories are not
followed.

.TP
.B \-\-auto
When the copy is done, do not finish: look at the sources again every
so often and copy whatever has changed, until interrupted.  With
\fB\-r\fP this keeps a directory at the destination up to date with one
at the source, in that one direction.  New and changed files are sent
whole.  A file that is still being written when it is looked at is sent
again once it has stopped changing.

.TP
.B \-\-delete
Also remove from the destination what is not at the source: once at the
start, within the directories being copied, and with \fB\-\-auto\fP as
things are removed from the source.

.TP
.B \-\-interval=\fISECONDS\fP
How often \fB\-\-auto\fP looks for changes.  The default is 2.

.TP
.B \-q
Say only what goes wrong.

.PP
The options of
.BR mosh (1)
that concern the connection also apply, among them \fB\-\-ssh\fP,
\fB\-\-server\fP, \fB\-p\fP and \fB\-\-relay\fP.

.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if everything asked for was copied, 1 if something was not.  What was
not, and why, is said on standard error, whichever end it happened at.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mocp report.pdf server:docs/
mocp \-r server:build/out .
mocp \-\-auto \-r \-\-delete server:project/build/. ./build
mocp 'server:logs/*.txt' logs/
.fi

.SH NOTES
Symbolic links are copied as the files they point to.  Ownership,
special files and hard links are not kept.  Nothing is compressed.
Changed files are sent whole, not as differences;
.BR rsync (1)
can be run through a mosh session with \fBrsync \-e 'mosh \-\-stdio'\fP
where that matters.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mosh (1),
.BR scp (1).

